    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good point about the relation field. My main concern is when the attribution feels almost \"otherworldly\" or just completely nonsensical—like when the marks are so strange they don't even resemble standard 19th-century cartographic signatures. In cases where the provenance feels totally disconnected from known historical patterns, how do we categorize those \"outlier\" creators without making the metadata look like it's filled with errors or nonsense?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For truly inexplicable or \"outlier\" marks, it is best to resist assigning any creator attribution altogether for those specific items. Instead, ensure the `dc:contributor` or a `dc:rights` note clearly states \"Attribution Unidentifiable/Non-Standard,\" focusing the metadata entirely on the object's physical description. This signals to the researcher that the attribution is deliberately withheld due to lack of evidence, preventing the metadata from appearing erroneous.",
      "source": "target",
